{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"ore-wretched-nene","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# The hoisted overlap-judge prefix is 149-296 tokens short of the 1024 cache floor\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nStory `8415-b6e8-c206-48f6` moved the batch-invariant query digest out of the per-call user message\nand into the overlap-judge system prompt, so the shared bytes sit in the block the runner already\nputs a cache breakpoint on. That relocation is done and measured. It does not yet pay off, because\nthe resulting prefix does not reach the model's minimum cacheable prefix.\n\nMeasured on real `anthropic:claude-sonnet-4-6` calls (the `standard` class `overlap-judge` runs on\npost-`afeb`), over one query ticket and six candidate digests, 12 calls:\n\n    stable system prefix   before  423 tokens        after  846 tokens      floor  1024\n    input tokens           before  16,040            after  16,976          (+936, +5.8%)\n    cache_read_tokens      before  0                 after  0\n    cache_write_tokens     before  0                 after  0\n    verdict parity         relations identical on 12/12 ordered pairs\n\nThe floor itself was measured, not assumed: through the real judge request path, an 875-token\nsystem prefix produced write 0 / read 0 while a 1250-token one produced write 1243 then read 1243.\n`count_tokens` over the base prompt (423) plus each of seven real `rebar.llm.enrich` digests\n(306-453) gives 728-875 — every one short of 1024 by **149-296 tokens**. So today the relocation\ncosts ~5.8% more input and returns nothing, and it will keep returning nothing until the stable\nprefix crosses the floor.\n\n## Approach\n\nClose the gap by cutting call volume rather than by padding the prompt (padding to reach a cache\nfloor buys the cache with the tokens it was meant to save). Batch several candidates into a single\njudge call: one call carrying the system prefix plus N candidate digests instead of N calls each\ncarrying the prefix plus one. That attacks the total from both ends at once — it removes N-1\ncopies of the prefix outright, and the surviving prompt is large enough that the prefix clears 1024\nand begins to cache for the calls that remain.\n\nThe cost is precision, and it is a real cost, not a formality. `reviewers/overlap_judge.md` is\nwritten for ONE ORDERED PAIR — \"FIRST <relation> SECOND\", a closed five-value relation set, a\nrequired named `shared_artifact`, `related_distinct` as the default, and \"false flags are costly\".\nJudging several candidates in one call risks cross-contamination between them. Any batching change\nmust therefore demonstrate that the same candidate set yields the same relations as the per-pair\nimplementation, using the parity harness story 8415 already established, and must keep the step\nadvisory and fail-safe: one malformed batch must degrade to abstain for that batch, never fail a\nreview.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/overlap/judge.py` — batch construction and per-candidate response demultiplexing.\n- `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/overlap_judge.md` — the prompt must describe a batch of SECOND\n  candidates against one FIRST, and must emit one verdict per candidate.\n- `src/rebar/llm/contracts.py` and the `overlap_verdict` output schema — a batched response shape.\n- `tests/unit/test_overlap_judge.py` — batch payload shape, demultiplexing, and fail-safe tests.\n\n## Testing\n\n- Payload shape: a recording-runner test asserts one `RunRequest` per batch carrying every\n  candidate's digest, each labelled with an identifier the response can be keyed back to.\n- Demultiplexing: a canned batched response maps back to exactly one verdict per candidate, in the\n  right order, with an unmatched or missing entry treated as abstain.\n- Fail-safe: a runner that raises, and a runner that returns a malformed batch, both yield `[]`\n  from `judge()` with nothing propagating.\n- Real-API parity: the same query ticket and candidate corpus judged per-pair and batched, relations\n  compared pair by pair.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The batched build issues strictly fewer calls than candidates x 2 for a corpus of >= 6\n      candidates, shown by a recording-runner test counting `RunRequest`s.\n- [ ] Every candidate receives exactly one verdict, and an absent or unparseable per-candidate entry\n      becomes an abstain; checked by the demultiplexing tests.\n- [ ] The step stays advisory: a raising runner and a malformed batched response each yield `[]`\n      from `judge()` with no exception escaping; checked by the fail-safe tests.\n- [ ] Precision is shown, not assumed: a real-API parity run over one query ticket and at least six\n      candidates reports per-pair `relation` for the per-pair build and the batched build, and any\n      differing relation is called out with its digests; recorded as a ticket comment.\n- [ ] A BEFORE/AFTER measurement on real calls records total `input_tokens`, `cache_read_tokens`,\n      `cache_write_tokens` and the `count_tokens` size of the stable prefix, for both builds. The\n      batched build must show a lower `input_tokens` total than 16,976 on the same corpus, and\n      `cache_write_tokens` no greater than one prefix write per batch.\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and `pytest -m \"not integration and not external\"` all exit 0.","id":"c403-cc3a-2c6d-4b9d","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Batch overlap-judge candidates so the shared prefix clears the 1024-token cache floor"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785710921240898001,"uuid":"b653a385-2f2a-49f8-8f41-1172c73d8874"}